045417] [17592186105058 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186105061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105058 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186105062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105058 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186105063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105058 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105058 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105058 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186105059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105058 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d9bbd5d1-52fc-5532-9c11-a0ca2391cb50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105059 #edb/ref 1080 "2.75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105059 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105059 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8e0e2534-557c-5d1d-a4c1-a63a3979c0e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105060 #edb/ref 1080 "3.5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105060 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105060 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a5dd965c-513e-5c45-be86-386b4fde7bdc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105061 #edb/ref 1080 "2.5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105061 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105061 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c0f8f7a3-d0f4-5000-b70c-55ece98dc00f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105062 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105062 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105062 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e569548f-e5e4-5a19-a049-83ace359cba6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105063 #edb/ref 1080 "3.25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105063 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105063 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "59352711-4b84-54f5-8f21-16affdcef33e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105577 #edb/ref 1069 "e-392" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105577 #edb/ref 1071 "Estimate the area under the curve $y=(2x-4)^2+2$ over the interval $[0,2]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $\\Delta x=0.5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105577 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2eb23241-bd2b-5b76-b885-25cd3a51c16d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105577 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/94/94618262fb303035cd03ccf0274694d7083d9cd3a23d4231e3a8fccde8ac4fe1.png)\n\n \n\nThe area $\\mathcal{A}$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(0.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(2)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $11$ | $6$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(11+6+3+2)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =22\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105578 #edb/ref 1069 "e-394" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105578 #edb/ref 1071 "The following table shows the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[1,4].$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $3.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0$ | $4$ | $8.5$ | $10$ | $8$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum, with the four subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[1,4].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105578 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "41e80d7f-8a72-5bbf-9f66-c0047b28e45b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105578 #edb/ref 1074 "In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is computed as \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}\t\\approx\tf(2)\\Delta x_1+f(3)\\Delta x_2+f(3.5)\\Delta x_3+f(4)\\Delta x_4,\n$$\n\nwhere each $\\Delta x_i$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\Delta x_i = x_{i}-x_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a table of values that include the $\\Delta x$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $3.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\Delta x$ | $-$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0.5$ | $0.5$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $4$ | $8.5$ | $10$ | $8$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈4\\cdot 1+8.5\\cdot 1+10\\cdot 0.5+8\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =4+8.5+5+4 \\\\  & =21.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105648 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7231" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105648 #edb/ref 1071 "The table below gives some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ on the closed interval $[-4,2].$ The right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ equal subintervals has a value of $12.$ What must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $3$ | $k$ | $2$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105648 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c1deb898-8696-537f-8fb2-ae0f354dec29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105648 #edb/ref 1074 "The area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula for the right Riemann sum:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(-2)}}+{\\color{red}{f(0)}}+{\\color{red}{f(2)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}\n$$\n\nSo, we set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $k$ | $2$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3+k+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(k+5)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that $\\mathcal A$ must be $12,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n2k+10 & =12\\[5pt]\n      2k &= 12-10\\[5pt]\n        k &= \\dfrac{2}{2}\\[5pt]\n         k  &= 1.\n}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105649 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7233" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105649 #edb/ref 1071 "Estimate the area under the curve $y=(2x-4)^2+2$ over the interval $[0,2]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $\\Delta x=0.5.$ Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105649 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1f74cbf1-c3f5-5ac2-ad55-ad22d7dae3ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105649 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/94/94618262fb303035cd03ccf0274694d7083d9cd3a23d4231e3a8fccde8ac4fe1.png)\n\n \n\nThe area $\\mathcal{A}$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(0.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(2)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $11$ | $6$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(11+6+3+2)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =22\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly decreasing* over the interval $[0,2],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105721 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105721 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105721 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105721 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105721 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105721 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105721 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105721 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105721 #edb/ref 1038 #e