/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105959 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105959 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105959 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105959 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105959 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105959 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105959 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105959 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105959 #edb/ref 1045 "Approximating Areas With the Right Riemann Sum" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105959 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186106598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105959 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105959 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105959 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f5bc6246-bbe1-5e71-a301-e64564d76448" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106369 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186104362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106369 #edb/ref 1069 "q-49117" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106369 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106369 #edb/ref 1071 "A right Riemann sum is used to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=-3$ and $x=-1$ for each of the functions below. For which function does the Riemann sum give an *overestimate* of the area?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106369 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa1b07db-8546-5dcc-a57c-3d4bbe55271b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106369 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106369 #edb/ref 1074 "The right Riemann sum overestimates the area under the respective curve if the function is increasing over the given interval. \n\nOf the given options, the only function that is increasing over $x\\in [-3,-1]$ is as follows.\n\n![](images/4d/4d4e96c9dc96dcfc8ef6c2fe984a915ff047531ce33706dd781a9d159ca82950.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106485 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186105058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106485 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74324" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106485 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106485 #edb/ref 1071 "Estimate the area under the curve $y=4-2x$ over the interval $[0,1]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.25.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106485 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7d9f3e81-c696-5e30-a1a6-32de40a197d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106485 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106485 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/c4/c478dd4988aaf23348c2453e1f9fca137ddd33284cde7cb7eae1775982bd4b2e.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0.25)+f(0.5)+f(0.75)+f(1)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.75$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3.5$ | $3$ | $2.5$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3.5+3+2.5+2)\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =11\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =2.75\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106598 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106598 #edb/ref 1061 646701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106598 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "39a0db46-10ca-5ca8-80cd-7262b7bef7bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106598 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106599 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106599 #edb/ref 1061 646703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106599 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5ad9803e-7cb4-50f5-8c8e-7a9260fa6ed5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106599 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106600 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106600 #edb/ref 1061 646702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106600 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4b4ff77f-3654-5883-84bd-bc2a0712cbe5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106600 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106601 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106601 #edb/ref 1061 646713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106601 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7d5f671d-e0d4-5b0a-8317-1304ba7d929f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106601 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106602 #edb/ref 1061 646714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106602 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e15fac05-66ad-5f49-9bed-51db8cc7f71c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106602 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106753 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106753 #edb/ref 1061 646706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106753 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f11aa5d6-b2b3-51c0-8bd1-0228ad7b133b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106753 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106754 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106754 #edb/ref 1061 646709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106754 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "efefd8b4-c7ee-50d3-abef-483a7d25557b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106754 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106755 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106755 #edb/ref 1061 646710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106755 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a49d1998-d787-5d44-a81a-b54f11630c6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106755 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107100 #edb/ref 1064 "Whether the right Riemann sum underestimates or overestimates the actual area under a function depends on whether the function is increasing or decreasing.\n\n- If the function $f(x)$ is increasing, then the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means the right Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the actual area under the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/71/71bc3147a2d7ee5867740e67f55d2a257065f0f225b62b4af3b5f5990c0d4ffc.png)\n\n- If the function $f(x)$ is decreasing, then  the rectangles fall *below* the function, which means the right Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the actual area under the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/0b/0bc5bc2d5daa2b42e640d31b998707af503b4c4489531ef7ddd41f4374405ef7.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107100 #edb/ref 1065 4373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107100 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ca191bef-f207-5cb0-88a1-6c46288c5c09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107100 #edb/ref 1067 "Overestimates and Underestimates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107150 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we wish to find the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve $y=x^2$ between the $x$-values $x=1$ and $x=4,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/35/3538fb94de9aac062e7ed65b9496085d478be911e5da2bf8480b260cec70ae4f.png)\n\n \n\nWe can approximate the area using, for instance, the area of three rectangles, each with a width equal to $1.$\n\n![](images/cb/cbb7b5af27aa5ebd3b5bbb51026017454999682dcf7e172411df79278564a5ae.png)\n\nSo, we find the area of each rectangle and then add them all together.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\overset{\\text{[math]1[/math]st rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(2)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{base}}\\,]}}+\\overset{\\text{[math]2[/math]nd rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(3)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{base}}\\,]}}+\\overset{\\text{[math]3[/math]rd rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(4)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{base}}\\,]}} \\\\  & =[{2}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{3}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{4}^{2}\\cdot 1] \\\\  & =4+9+16 \\\\  & =29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we approximated the area using a **right Riemann sum**, which means that the top-*right* corner of each rectangle touches the curve.\n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,4],$ the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the actual area (see the picture above)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107150 #edb/ref 1065 733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107150 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a8dc0c69-e0be-5d9c-bb2a-4838709fb9eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107150 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107151 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[a,b]$ can be approximated using a right Riemann sum by calculating the area of each rectangle and adding them together. \n\n![](images/6a/6aa3fd6dca84719aa7d34fdf75586a33005dd34c416d4456662f9d1876679f61.png)\n\nIn the image, we assume that $x_0=a$ and $x_n=b.$  \n\nThe resulting area can be calculated as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}\t\\approx f(x_1)\\Delta x+f(x_2)\\Delta x+f(x_3)\\Delta x+ \\cdots + f(x_n) \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of rectangles and the step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta x=\\dfrac{b-a}{n}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify by factoring out the $\\Delta x,$ which gives the following general formula:\n\n![](images/85/8570545c3437ee862deba766b58f5746629452459505488a4c59ca17869aa95c.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107151 #edb/ref 1065 735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107151 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "09dcf1d5-eddf-58d0-8f4b-eeae9cf26024" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107151 #edb/ref 1067 "A General Formula for the Right Riemann Sum" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107152 #edb/ref 1064 "In the last few examples, the step size $\\Delta{x}$ was the same for each of the rectangles. In these cases, we say that the step size is **regular**.\n\nThere are situations where the step size can vary, which we call an **irregular step size**. But we can still use a right Riemann sum to approximate the area.\n\nFor example, let's use a right Riemann sum to approximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n\nThe first thing we note is that the domain of the function $y=f(x)$ is *partitioned* as \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n [1,4] &= [1, 1.5] \\cup [1.5, 2.5] \\cup [2.5, 4].\n}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](images/60/60c0bfba52fe087b4d8aaeceee367798e24079dc385afff3e83bca60405d4d2b.png)\n\n  \n\nWe've drawn some rectangles to help us calculate our right Riemann sum, and we have chosen the heights of each rectangle to be calculated using the right endpoint of each interval.\n\nBy calculating the area of each rectangle, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n![](images/14/142608196dc9a4498e79c7b5b15850401489b08e0ba658316d14ccf05b47728a.svg)\n\nPlugging our numbers into the above, we find\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\mathcal{A}\t&\\approx\t{\\color{red}2} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}1.5-1})\n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ {\\color{red}6} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}2.5-1.5})\n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ {\\color{red}8} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}4-2.5}) \\ \n\t\t\t&= {\\color{red}2} \\cdot {\\color{blue}0.5} +{\\color{red}6} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1}+ {\\color{red}8} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1.5}  \\\n\t\t\t&=1+6+12\\\n\t\t\t&= 19.\n}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107152 #edb/ref 1065 736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107152 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b662a09b-80ce-5d1d-8950-1d407892ead3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107152 #edb/ref 1067 "Right Riemann Sums with Irregular Step Size" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339324 #edb/ref 1080 "29.25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339324 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339324 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1649648