following table shows the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[4,9].$\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $5.5$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $4$ | $6$ | $9.5$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum, with the three subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[4,9].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342196 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dc8544c2-610f-58ff-bd57-2903f678a6bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342196 #edb/ref 1074 "In this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA≈f(5.5)Δ{x}_{1}+f(8)Δ{x}_{2}+f(9)Δ{x}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $5.5$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $-$ | $1.5$ | $2.5$ | $1$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $4$ | $6$ | $9.5$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈4\\cdot 1.5+6\\cdot 2.5+9.5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6+15+9.5 \\\\  & =30.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535435 #edb/ref 1080 "$7,$ overestimate" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535435 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535435 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "002a6a31-b58c-5580-9148-3001495a4d7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535437 #edb/ref 1080 "$2\\,220$ flour bags" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535437 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535437 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "01050c59-9553-589f-9c48-f5d7d72b8958" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535449 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535449 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535449 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "07357d7a-2f83-5eb7-92b8-b510a24224cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] 